  "notes": "THE ANNOTATION FIXTURE (specs/03 §12, KB §D.5). A blank ACORD 25 (2010/05) that Durham County has over-printed in red and orange to show a vendor what a real certificate must carry. IT IS LABELLED EXACTLY AS PRINTED, box by box, with `value` null wherever the printed characters are not a value of that field's type. That rule needs no judgement about ink colour and it is the same rule every other fixture is labelled under — and it is what makes this fixture bite: the POLICY EFF and POLICY EXP boxes print the sentence 'Must not be expired', so their raw is that sentence and their value is null; the umbrella row's POLICY NUMBER box prints 'May be used to meet min. limits on General Liabitlity and Auto Liability.' (the county's own typo, kept), so its value is null too. An extractor that turns either into a date or a policy number has done exactly what specs/03 §12 warns about. The DESCRIPTION OF OPERATIONS box contains the county's TIPS block; it is a free-text box, so its printed content is its value, verbatim. The numbered red guidance in the margins is outside every box and is not labelled. 'CONTRACTOR'S INSURANCE BROKER' is printed in black as part of the form; 'CONTRACTOR NAME' and 'DURHAM COUNTY' are printed in the insured and certificate-holder boxes and are labelled as printed. No insurer row carries a name, and the authorised-representative box is unsigned. PERSONAL DATA: none. Every name on this document is an organisation or a placeholder.",
